{"parentUuid":"d1db71e2-62ed-4484-9e60-89d2a0b94d3c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9bdfc07b-8ff1-4e95-9c88-de7440fd6192","agentId":"aea371135e52bab86","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GYYXVnhatvDDiSP2sJ6nME","type":"tool_result","content":"engine-architect :: Owns the engine's overall architecture: requirements and configuration profiles, layering and module boundaries, the contract registry, global conventions, build/integrate/buy decisions and cross-domain arbitration. Final authority on the boundaries between skills. || Running the ADR, fitness-function and anti-legacy process | Scheduling work across agents and the ownership ledger | Architecture inside a domain (e.g. shading-path choice)\n\nprogram-orchestration :: Turns architecture into coordinated multi-agent work: decomposes milestones into tasks by skill territory, keeps the ownership ledger and territory locks, sequences integration through vertical slices, routes contract change requests, and schedules critic gates. || Deciding architecture | CI mechanics | Release versioning\n\nperformance-architect :: Owns performance as a discipline: the hypothesis → instrumentation → measurement → optimization → regression-test loop, budgets per subsystem and hardware tier, the scalability / device-profile framework, and performance gates. || Optimizing subsystem code (via C-PERF findings) | Instrumentation API | Benchmark harness | Runtime scalability machinery\n\ncpu-performance :: CPU microarchitecture-level optimization and review: cache and memory-access patterns, vectorization audits, contention and false sharing, hybrid-core and power-aware tuning, PGO/LTO. || Owning subsystem code | Math library | Scheduler design | Changing subsystem code (findings route via C-PERF)\n\ngpu-performance :: GPU performance analysis and review: occupancy, bandwidth, cache behavior, barrier and async-compute overlap, vendor tools across PC, console and mobile, GPU power. || Render graph scheduling design | Shader authoring for features\n\nloading-streaming-performance :: End-to-end load time, startup time and hitch elimination (PSO compilation, streaming stalls, GC pauses, synchronous waits), IO throughput analysis. || IO backend implementation | PSO cache implementation | Streaming policy\n\nfunctional-automation-soak :: Game-level automation: scripted and bot playthroughs, smoke/boot tests, soak and stability runs (memory growth, leaks), hardware/OS compatibility matrix, network harnesses and server load tests. || Unit test frameworks | Device-farm infrastructure\n\ndeterminism-replay :: Defines determinism levels (none, same-binary, cross-platform) per subsystem and the floating-point and parallel-execution rules that achieve them; owns input/state record-replay and desync detection. || Rollback netcode | Deterministic math implementation\n\ncore-runtime-architect :: Core runtime architecture: layering within core, module system and engine lifecycle (parallel boot, init graph, shutdown), configuration and cvars, error-handling and failure model, language and compiler feature policy. || Allocator design | Scheduler | Frame loop\n\nmemory-allocators :: Memory management: virtual memory, allocator families, thread-local and NUMA-aware allocation, tagging and attribution, budget enforcement mechanisms, fragmentation control, memory debugging, OOM policy, UMA accounting. || GPU heaps | Budget numbers\n\ncontainers-core-types :: Containers and core types: cache-friendly containers (open-addressing maps, inline and SoA containers, rings), strings and UTF-8, interned names, hashing, bit utilities, algorithms, explicit-ownership and view types. || Concurrent containers | Entity handles\n\nconcurrency-primitives :: Memory model and atomics, synchronization primitives, lock-free and wait-free structures, safe memory reclamation, thread-safety annotations and model checking; reviewer for concurrent code engine-wide. || Scheduling | Concurrency test campaigns\n\njob-system-task-graph :: Task-parallel execution: work-stealing scheduler, dependency task graphs, fibers and C++20 coroutines, priorities and QoS, heterogeneous-core and NUMA placement, IO/GPU completion integration, parallel algorithms, blocking policy. || Frame phase layout | IO backends\n\nframe-orchestration :: The engine loop expressed as a task graph: frame pipelining across simulation, render and GPU, tick phases and sync points, fixed-step simulation with interpolation, time domains, latency and pacing control. || System ordering inside a phase | Swapchain presentation\n\necs-runtime :: ECS: storage (archetype chunks or sparse sets, chosen by ADR), queries, change detection, structural changes and command buffers, system scheduling with access-conflict analysis, relationships, singletons, authoring → runtime baking, debugging. || World partition | Transform propagation | Object-model policy\n\nreflection-metadata :: Type registry, reflection generation (codegen now, C++26 static reflection when toolchains allow), attributes, the property system, script-binding substrate, dynamic invocation. || Serialization formats | Editor widgets | Script VM\n\nplatform-services :: First-party platform and store services: identity and sign-in, achievements and presence, entitlements and commerce through first-party stores, privileges and parental controls, social graph and invites, block/mute and text filtering, crossplay policy, leaderboards, cloud-save APIs, media capture, age and region policy enforcement. || Third-party/own backend & live-ops boundaries | Game-server hosting | Save-game content | Backend implementation\n\ninput-system :: Action-level input: mapping and context stacks, rebinding, buffering and latency accounting, local-multiplayer assignment, accessibility input options, UI navigation routing, per-device glyphs, recording and replay. || Device drivers and haptics | UI focus logic\n\nasset-cook-processors :: Generic processors: texture processing and GPU texture-compression encoding, mesh optimization, hosting of collision and other domain builders under the cook contract. || Cluster DAG building | Animation compression | Shader compilation\n\nasync-io-storage :: Async IO: io_uring, IOCP, DirectStorage and console backends, IO scheduling and queue depth, GPU decompression, memory-mapped IO, file-system abstraction, error handling. || Streaming decisions | Codec choice\n\nworld-architect :: World representation strategy (data-oriented, no universal scene graph), partitioning and streaming cells, streaming sources, HLOD strategy, simulation LOD and significance, large-world coordinate policy, level transitions, server-side world streaming. || World document format | Transform math | HLOD mesh generation\n\nworld-data-model :: World and level documents, prefabs with nested overrides and variants, data layers, one-file-per-object layout for collaboration, level instances. || Serialization mechanics | Source-control workflows\n\nterrain :: Terrain end-to-end: representation, rendering, material layering, sculpt/paint tool logic, runtime deformation, collision data hand-off. || Virtual texturing core | Viewport hosting | Collision algorithms\n\nrender-architect :: Rendering architecture: pipeline organization, shading-path choice (forward+, deferred, visibility buffer), render-feature contract, render scene and world→render sync, scalability tiers from 2D mobile to path-traced, multi-view, debug modes, render threading. || API backends | Pass scheduling | Individual features (GI, shadows…)\n\nrhi-core :: Render hardware interface over D3D12, Vulkan, Metal, console APIs and WebGPU: queues and command recording, synchronization, bindless descriptors, PSOs and PSO caching, presentation, capability tiers, GPU crash breadcrumbs, driver workarounds. || GPU memory allocation | Barrier planning | Shader compilation | Per-API backend code\n\nrender-graph-scheduling :: Render graph declaration and compilation, automatic barriers, transient aliasing, async compute and multi-queue scheduling, TBDR pass merging, history resources, work-graph integration, general GPU compute, readback contract. || Heap allocation | Feature passes\n\nshader-system :: Shading language and dialect, compilation toolchain, permutations, reflection and binding layouts, interop headers, caches and distributed compilation, hot reload, shader debugging, PSO-list gathering. || Material graphs | Runtime PSO cache\n\nvirtualized-geometry-lod :: All geometric LOD: cluster hierarchies and virtualized geometry, cluster DAG building, software micro-polygon rasterization, cluster streaming, discrete LOD, HLOD proxies and impostors, deforming-geometry LOD, displacement. || HLOD strategy | Generic mesh optimization | RT acceleration structures\n\ntexture-streaming-vt :: Runtime texture formats, mip streaming, sampler-feedback streaming, virtual texturing, streaming-friendly cooked layout; evaluates neural texture compression. || Texture encoding in cook | Streaming manager\n\ndirect-lighting-shadows :: Light types and physical units, clustered light culling, stochastic many-light sampling, area lights, cascaded, virtual and ray-traced shadows, contact shadows, light profiles. || Indirect lighting | Denoisers\n\nglobal-illumination :: Indirect lighting across tiers: baked lightmaps, probe volumes, screen-space GI and AO, surface/radiance-cache hybrid GI, ReSTIR GI, reflections, sky lighting, specular occlusion, SDF scene representations. || RT infrastructure | Atmosphere model\n\nphysics-architect :: Physics architecture: build vs integrate decision, physics world representation and ECS integration, stepping and frame sync, determinism levels, physics LOD, materials, GPU offload evaluation; covers both 2D and 3D. || Collision algorithms | Solvers | Networked physics\n\nanimation-architect :: Animation architecture: data model, parallel evaluation pipeline, animation/physics/gameplay sync and root-motion policy, animation LOD, budgets. || Sampling and blending | Skinning\n\nfacial-animation :: Facial rigs and solvers, audio-driven and ML lip sync, performance-capture import. || Face rendering | Dialogue playback\n\naudio-architect :: Audio engine architecture and threading, device backends, voice management and virtualization, middleware decision, audio budgets. || DSP algorithms | Spatialization\n\nnetwork-architect :: Netcode model per game type (authoritative server, lockstep, rollback, P2P/relay), network tick and time, authority model, budgets and scale targets, protocol versioning, sharded worlds evaluation. || Transport | Replication mechanics | Matchmaking services\n\nprediction-rollback :: Client prediction and reconciliation, rollback netcode, lag compensation, deterministic lockstep, networked physics, clock synchronization. || Determinism rules | Physics solver\n\ndedicated-server :: Headless server builds and stripping, tick cost and multi-instance density, fleet orchestration boundary, server observability, listen servers and host migration. || Fleet operation (outside engine scope) | Server logging substrate\n\ngameplay-architect :: Gameplay framework: rules, players and possession, extension points and game-module structure, game flow and transitions, local players. || Abilities/tags/volumes toolkit | Scripting VM | Game-specific code\n\ngameplay-systems-toolkit :: Reusable gameplay systems: abilities, effects and attributes, gameplay tags, camera system, spawning and pooling, gameplay message routing, photo mode. || Generic events | Cinematic cameras\n\nscripting-runtime :: Scripting language selection, VM embedding and bindings, sandboxing, debugging and profiling, hot reload, visual scripting, AOT/JIT constraints, GC policy. || Reflection substrate | Graph editor UI\n\npersistence-save :: Save-game model and serialization, save versioning and migration, atomic writes, platform save APIs and quotas, cloud-save sync, persistent world-state deltas, checkpoints. || Schema evolution mechanism | Cloud service API\n\nui-architect :: UI architecture (retained with data binding for game UI, immediate for developer UI), layout, styling, focus and navigation, animation, in-world UI, designer tool logic, UI performance. || UI draw backend | Text shaping | Editor panels\n\ntext-fonts :: Font loading and fallback, glyph rasterization, shaping, bidi and line breaking, rich text and emoji, text input and IME. || String content | 2D draw submission\n\nlocalization-i18n :: String tables, ICU-class message formatting, CLDR locale formatting, localization pipeline, pseudo-localization, localized asset variants, culturalization. || Glyph rendering | VO playback\n\neditor-ui-framework :: Tool UI toolkit (docking, panels), reflection-driven inspectors, asset browser, immediate-mode developer UI, editor UX standards. || Game UI | Graph editors\n\ngraph-editor-framework :: Shared node-graph editor for material, animation, VFX, audio, PCG and visual-script graphs: framework, compile/validation contract, debugging, diff and merge. || Each domain's graph semantics\n\ncollaboration-version-control :: Source-control integration, locking and checkout, multi-user concurrent editing, merge tooling for scene and asset data, content review, large-binary storage strategy. || Branching model | File-per-object layout\n\nruntime-scalability :: Runtime scalability machinery: device-profile application, the registry of scalability knobs and actuators, first-run hardware auto-detect, and the closed-loop runtime governor that consumes frame-time, thermal/power and memory-pressure signals and drives registered actuators (dynamic resolution, significance, VFX, animation rate, worker count) with priorities and hysteresis. || Budget numbers and tier definitions | Hardware→tier device database | Each actuator's own implementation\n\nvoxel-worlds :: Chunked voxel/block worlds end to end: sparse storage and compression, meshing and remeshing on edit, runtime edit replication and persistence hand-off, block light propagation. || Player construction gameplay (snapping, structural integrity) | Heightfield terrain | Replication mechanics\n\ngpu-platform-architect :: GPU platform architecture shared by every renderer tier: layering of RHI, GPU memory, render graph, shader system, ray-query and inference infrastructure; the GPU feature-tier model (binding, pipeline-state, queue, mesh, RT, tensor tiers) and API baselines per hardware tier; task-based parallel command recording. || Rendering pipeline and features | Individual API backends | Frames in flight / thread existence\n\nrhi-vulkan :: Implements C-RHI on Vulkan (profiles incl. Android Baseline, descriptor buffers/heaps, synchronization2, dynamic rendering); owns its validation-layer runs and vendor/driver quirks. || RHI abstraction & capability model | Other API backends\n\nrhi-webgpu :: Implements C-RHI on WebGPU (bind groups, implicit sync, limits tiers); owns its validation-layer runs and vendor/driver quirks. || RHI abstraction & capability model | Other API backends\n\nmedia-playback :: Pre-rendered video playback end to end: hardware/software decode backends, A/V sync against the audio clock, video surfaces for UI, world and cinematics, HDR video, package streaming. || Movie rendering (producing video) | Audio mixing\n\ngameplay-data :: Designer-owned data: typed data tables and row references, curve assets, tuning assets with inheritance and per-platform/difficulty variants, spreadsheet round-trip, balance simulation, and live data hotfixes through remote overrides. || Serialization mechanics | Remote-config service | Tabular editing widgets\n\nnarrative-dialogue :: The dialogue line model and narrative runtime shared by VO, subtitles, lip sync, localization, cinematics and AI: line database with stable IDs, branching dialogue runtime and narrative-middleware interchange, narrative fact store with save/replication participation, barks and response rules, recording scripts and placeholder VO. || Quest content | VO playback | String formatting\n\nreference-games :: The reference-game ladder (one representative game per configuration) used as milestone and release gate, representative production-scale content sets, and the internal playtest and dogfood loop. || Teaching samples | Benchmark harness\n\nplatform-web :: Web target (WASM + WebGPU): threads and cross-origin isolation, memory limits, OPFS/IndexedDB storage, HTTP streaming delivery, user-gesture rules for audio/pointer/fullscreen, browser transport constraints. || WebGPU backend | Browser transports\n\nmemory-performance :: Memory as its own performance discipline: whole-process CPU+GPU+UMA footprint analysis per tier, snapshot capture and diffing, content-level attribution per asset/cell/feature, transition peak analysis, fragmentation over time; reviewer for the memory-usage obligation. || Allocator mechanisms | Budget numbers\n\nsimulation-validation :: Runs the objective validation that domain owners define for simulation-class systems: physics stability and reference suites, simulation golden traces, animation error suites, audio offline-render suites, and deterministic network simulation for prediction/replication correctness, mirroring what render-validation does for rendering. || Defining each domain's oracles | Determinism matrix definition","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5dc8f029-2c25-4489-9258-4f0c791fd226","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:22:11.858Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d1db71e2-62ed-4484-9e60-89d2a0b94d3c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1"}
